name: xargs and doing it to everything
boss: Swarm
flavor: A thousand commands. One directive.
brief: Some commands read filenames as arguments; pipes carry text on stdin.
brief: xargs is the adaptor between them: it turns lines of input into
brief: arguments of a command.
brief:
brief: The classic trap is filenames with spaces. find -print0 emits names
brief: separated by a NUL byte, which cannot occur in a filename, and xargs -0
brief: reads them back. Use that pair by default and the problem never arises.
---
setup: a pile of .log files has built up and you want line counts for all of them
q: %find% every .log file and count the lines of each
a: find . -name '*.log' | xargs wc -l
cmp: ref
ref: xargs
teach: xargs
hint: xargs turns lines of stdin into arguments appended to the command
---
q: {count the lines of every .log file, safe for names with spaces|count each .log file's lines in a way that survives spaces in names|line-count every .log file here, spaces in the names and all}
a: find . -name '*.log' -print0 | xargs -0 wc -l
cmp: ref
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -0
hint: NUL is the one byte a filename cannot contain
---
q: {a command only takes one argument at a time -- run it once per input line|feed the command one item per invocation, not all at once|run echo once per file instead of one giant invocation}
a: ls | xargs -n 1 echo
cmp: ref
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -n
hint: -n caps how many arguments go into each invocation
---
q: {you need each filename in the middle of the command, not tacked on the end|the argument has to land mid-command -- give xargs a placeholder for where|put each item wherever you want in the command, not just trailing}
a: ls *.log | xargs -I {} echo found {}
out: ^found .*\.log$
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -I
hint: -I defines a placeholder that gets replaced with each input item
---
q: %show% each command before running it
a: ls | xargs -t echo
cmp: ref
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -t
hint: -t prints the command line to stderr before running it
---
q: {a pipeline sometimes produces nothing -- don't run the command in that case|when there's no input at all, skip running the command entirely|don't let an empty input still trigger one empty invocation}
a: printf '' | xargs -r echo nothing
why: primitive-cost
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -r
hint: without -r, an empty input still runs the command once with no arguments
---
q: {the input has a STOP marker -- ignore everything from there on|cut off input the moment the word STOP shows up|treat STOP as the end of input, whatever follows it}
a: printf 'a b STOP c' | xargs -E STOP echo
cmp: ref
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -E
hint: -E names a string that, once seen, ends input reading right there
---
q: {these are independent and slow -- run 2 at a time instead of one by one|speed things up by running 2 invocations in parallel|don't wait for each one to finish before starting the next -- cap it at 2}
a: ls | xargs -P 2 -n 1 echo
why: primitive-cost
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -P
hint: -P sets how many invocations run in parallel at once
---
q: {this could go wrong -- confirm each command before it actually runs|don't trust the list blindly -- approve each invocation first|make xargs pause and ask before every command}
a: ls | xargs -p echo
why: interactive
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -p
hint: -p prompts for confirmation before each invocation, handy for a dry run
---
q: {the list is already saved in @txt@ -- use it, no piping needed|@txt@ already holds the arguments -- feed it straight in|read the argument list out of @txt@ instead of piping it in}
a: xargs -a @txt@ echo
out: rotate
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -a
hint: -a reads the argument list from a file instead of stdin
---
q: {some tool chokes on long command lines -- cap each one at 100 bytes|keep every generated command line under 100 bytes, splitting as needed|limit each invocation's command line to 100 bytes}
a: ls | xargs -s 100 echo
cmp: ref
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -s
hint: -s caps the total length of each generated command line
---
q: {you only want the filename out of @dir@/@txt@, not the whole path|find handed you @dir@/@txt@ -- reduce it down to just the filename|drop everything but the filename from @dir@/@txt@}
a: basename @dir@/@txt@
out: ^@txt@$
ref: basename
teach: basename
hint: basename strips everything up through the last slash
---
q: {strip @txt@ down to its name, dropping the .txt suffix too|get @txt@'s bare name with the .txt extension gone as well|reduce @txt@ to just its name, no directory and no .txt suffix}
a: basename @txt@ .txt
cmp: ref
ref: basename
teach: basename
hint: a second argument to basename is a suffix to strip as well
---
q: {strip .txt from @txt@ with the suffix named as a flag|name the .txt suffix explicitly stripping it off @txt@|drop .txt from @txt@ the way that still works with more files}
a: basename -s .txt @txt@
cmp: ref
ref: basename
teach: basename -s
hint: -s is the explicit-flag form of the same suffix stripping
---
q: {@txt@ and @log@ -- want just the filenames, in one call|process @txt@ and @log@ in one basename call, not two|reduce @txt@ and @log@ to bare filenames in one call}
a: basename -a @txt@ @log@
cmp: ref
ref: basename
teach: basename -a
hint: -a lets you hand basename several paths in one call
---
q: %show% the directory part of @dir@/@txt@
a: dirname @dir@/@txt@
out: ^@dir@$
ref: dirname
teach: dirname
hint: dirname is basename's mirror -- it keeps the directory and drops the name
---
q: {resolve @txt@ to its full absolute path|use realpath to turn @txt@ into an absolute path|find the absolute path realpath resolves @txt@ to}
a: realpath @txt@
cmp: ref
ref: realpath
teach: realpath
hint: realpath resolves any relative pieces and symlinks along the way
---
q: {follow @txt@ through symlinks to whatever it finally points at|use readlink -f to chase @txt@'s link chain to the end|resolve @txt@ all the way through any symlinks}
a: readlink -f @txt@
cmp: ref
ref: readlink
teach: readlink -f
hint: -f follows every link in the chain, not just the first one
---
q: {piping @txt@'s link target onward -- no stray newline allowed|@txt@'s target needs to come out with no trailing newline|feed @txt@'s link target on without an extra newline}
a: readlink -n @txt@
why: primitive-cost
ref: readlink
teach: readlink -n
hint: -n suppresses the trailing newline readlink would otherwise print
---
q: {where does the command sed live|use which to find the path sed resolves to|ask which where sed lives on disk}
a: which sed
cmp: ref
ref: which
teach: which
hint: which searches PATH and reports the file it would run
---
q: {is sed a builtin, a file, or a function|ask type to classify what sed actually is|use type to find out what kind of command sed is}
a: type sed
cmp: ref
ref: type
teach: type
hint: type reports whether a name is a builtin, a file, a function or an alias
---
setup: you're about to call sed hundreds of times in a loop
q: {stop the shell from searching PATH for sed every single time|cache sed's location so future calls skip the PATH search|make the shell remember where sed is instead of looking it up again}
a: hash sed
why: primitive-cost
ref: hash
teach: hash
hint: hash remembers a command's location so future lookups skip the PATH search
---
q: {check whether sed is available, the portable way|use command -v to check for sed portably, across shells|test whether sed exists using the POSIX-portable command -v}
a: command -v sed
cmp: ref
ref: command
teach: command -v
hint: -v prints the resolved command, or nothing if it isn't found
